          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I smoke and I have been diagnosed with COPD.",
          "answer": "Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD) is a lung condition that develops over many years, often causing a chronic cough and sputum production. People sometimes mistake early symptoms for normal aging or being out of shape, which can hide the condition's progression (condition-copd). A diagnosis of COPD requires a breathing test called spirometry and is not based on symptoms alone (condition-copd).\n\nTobacco smoking is the main cause of COPD in many countries (condition-copd). Other factors can also contribute, such as exposure to biomass fuel smoke, occupational dusts, fumes, air pollution, and childhood respiratory infections (condition-copd).\n\nStopping smoking is the only intervention that has been shown to slow down the rate at which lung function declines in people with COPD (guideline-copd-diagnosis-and-stepwise-management).\n\nGiven your diagnosis, discussing smoking cessation strategies with your clinician is an important step. They can help you explore methods and support to quit smoking.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
